{
  "requirement": "If the accepted result cannot be deserialized into `R`, awaiting the builder\nreturns `ValidationError::Agent`.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "**Obligation.** `requirements.sdk.graph.agent-calls.judge.submission.decoding` has one obligation. Suppose the harness accepts a result, but it cannot be deserialized into `R`. Then awaiting the builder must return `ValidationError::Agent`.\n\n**How the harness and decoding work.** I checked this in the implementation only to see which test inputs reach the decode step.\n- `GraphSession::submit` in `crates/trust-but-verify/src/agent/tools.rs` accepts a submission once `submitted_value` approves the envelope. The envelope must contain exactly a `result` field. `submission_envelopes_require_one_result_field` confirms this. The harness never validates the `result` value against `R`.\n- `Ask::execute` in `crates/trust-but-verify/src/procedure.rs` then runs `serde_json::from_value(value)` on the accepted value and maps any failure to `ValidationError::Agent`.\n\n**Test mapping.** The test is `typed_submission_references_resolve_and_invalid_results_are_agent_errors` in `crates/trust-but-verify/src/agent/tests.rs`.\n- It submits `{\"result\":\"wrong\"}` and awaits `ctx.ask::<bool>()`. The envelope is valid, so the harness accepts it. The string cannot deserialize into `bool`. The test asserts `Err(ValidationError::Agent(_))`. This is the decoding-failure scenario the requirement names, and the assertion enforces the required error variant.\n- The other three loop cases are `{}`, `{\"other\":true}` and a result with an extra field. They fail at the envelope check, which belongs to `agent-harness.submission.invalid`. They are not needed for this requirement, and they do no harm.\n- The success path is covered too. `{\"result\":{\"decision\":\"Yes\"}}` decodes into `Answer`, and the test asserts `Decision::Yes`. This shows that decoding does not fail for valid results, so the error-path assertion is meaningful.\n\n**Gaps considered.**\n- An implementation that returned a different error variant, or panicked, on a type mismatch would be caught by the `Err(ValidationError::Agent(_))` match.\n- One mismatch class (a string where a `bool` is expected) is enough. The requirement does not distinguish kinds of decode failure, and the code path is the same single `from_value` call.\n\nI found no adequacy gap, and the test is marked with a `{test}` reference to the subject.",
